          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every integer $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every integer $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{3}^{n}\\ge (n+1)^{2}\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(2).$ To do this, we substitute $n=2$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+1)^{2}=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=2.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 2.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{k}\\ge (k+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{3}^{k+1}\\ge (k+2)^{2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{k+1} & ={3}^{k}\\cdot {3}^{1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\,\\,\\,\\,{{{3}^{k}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge \\,(k+1)^{2}} & \\ge 3(k+1)^{2} \\\\  & =3{k}^{2}+6k+3 \\\\  & =(k+2)^{2}+{{2{k}^{2}+2k-1}_{⏟}}_{>0} \\\\  & >(k+2)^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${3}^{n}\\ge (n+1)^{2}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$",
